Z||000000||Dan010||11-26-2001||10:40 PM||||Read, re-read and then re-re-read, searched and did the same, but could not get it in my head what it was trying to tell me exactly.<P>Well, tried to adjust it, and did move the slider to stop, toward the throttle connection, what a difference, (right or wrong). Shifts stronger (harder) not as soon,<BR>and down shifts easier.<P>Is there any thing I should watch out for, read about higher trans pressure if not set correctly,<BR>its an 86GT auto with 90,000 on odometer.<BR>Also, had front end checked out and was told that I need inner tie rod and lower ball joint for same side. Dont drive bad, of course this is my first and only Fiero I   had been around, so I don't have another to compare it to. Has a knock from this side when I hit bumps. Tried the 9:00-3:00 then 12:00-6:00 little play, but not much.<BR>Does this sound correct???<BR>est. for them to fix was $240.00 including alignment||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||11-28-2001||01:21 PM||||If you can put a gage on the test point, you may want to check it.  On my car, the trans wouldn't shift early with hardly any throttle.  I was just curious so I did it to see what the effect was and then adjusted it properly.  You may want to move it some more to see if you get the same effect and then move back to where it is now.  The problem with the running constantly at the higher pressure is that typically these transaxles are fairly old (milage & age) so they won't do very well for very long.<P>||||reg||1
